 on the ballot", "Prediction markets price Ager around 70% to win", "Ager's large fundraising edge ($3.1M raised, DCCC ad support)", "Thin and partly partisan polling in an R-leaning district (PVI R+5)"], "rationale": "The district's R+5 lean is offset by an open seat, a strong Democratic environment, and Ager's dominant fundraising, while markets and most raters tilt toward a narrow Democratic edge. The single recent poll (D-sponsored) shows a modest Ager lead, consistent with a low-single-digit Democratic margin.", "watch": "Any fresh independent polling or a shift in the national environment toward Republicans could quickly move this back to a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Chuck Edwards (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Western North Carolina's 11th District is an open seat this year, as Republican Chuck Edwards is not on the ballot despite winning renomination. Democrat Jamie Ager faces Republican Jennifer Balkcom, with a Libertarian also in the field. The district leans Republican by about five points, but the national environment and the open-seat dynamic narrow that gap.\n\nAger has raised over $3 million and holds a large cash advantage, and the DCCC has begun advertising on his behalf. Most expert ratings call the race a tossup, with one rating it Lean Democratic and another Lean Republican, while prediction markets give Ager roughly a 70 percent chance. Polling is sparse and partly partisan, leaving real uncertainty in both directions.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.5972, "margin": 1.39}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6249, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6236, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6239, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.5922, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.5614, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.6011, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.6565, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_d